 3. THE TRIGGER, AND A DEFECT IT EXPOSED IN OUR OWN CODE
------------------------------------------------------------
  trigger outcome as first recorded  = "not delivered", exit 224
  device state immediately after     = absent from `adb devices`
  device state for the next 10 min   = absent, never returned

The first reading was WRONG, and the contract is what caught it. Issuing
wipeData tears the ADB connection down as the wipe starts, so a non-zero
exit code is exactly what a wipe starting looks like. The provider graded
the trigger on its exit code and therefore recorded the run's most important
outcome backwards - as a failure, when the evidence says it fired.

Fixed the same day:
  - WipeOutcome is now tri-state: DELIVERED / NOT_ISSUED / INDETERMINATE
  - reachability is established BEFORE the trigger, so "absent to begin
    with" can no longer be confused with "absent because it is wiping"
  - INDETERMINATE maps to TRIGGER_OUTCOME_INDETERMINATE with
    isSuccess = false: the product claims neither outcome it cannot
    establish
  - regression test pins the exit-224 shape: a connection that dies as the
    wipe starts is INDETERMINATE, never a failure

------------------------------------------------------------
 4. WHAT IS AND IS NOT ESTABLISHED
------------------------------------------------------------
ESTABLISHED:
  - the product's pre-flight correctly identified an enrolled device
  - the wipe command was issued against a real enrolled device
  - the device left ADB at the moment of the trigger

NOT ESTABLISHED (at trigger time; see section 6, which supersedes this):
  - that the wipe completed. The AFTER-reading had not yet been taken,
    because a factory reset disables USB debugging and leaves the device in
    the setup wizard. Under the ratified contract the verdict was therefore
    PURGE_INDETERMINATE - correctly so, for the period it describes. It was
    never reported as a failure, and it is not one.
  - that the purge ran through the full SANITIZE_EXECUTE protocol chain. It
    did not: that path is gated on the entitlement flag, which is still off.
    What ran was the destructive provider execute() itself calls.
  - Tier B (contacts, media, accounts) was not taken. Those sources sit
    outside the supported command set by deliberate design.

------------------------------------------------------------
 5. WHAT THE OPERATOR MUST DO BEFORE THE AFTER-READING
------------------------------------------------------------
Complete setup on the handset and re-enable USB debugging. Until the device
reappears in `adb devices`, no Tier A after-reading exists and the correct
verdict remains PURGE_INDETERMINATE - an inconclusive run, not a purge that
failed.

Note for every future purge: the wipe removes the agent, so the next purge
requires installing and enrolling the agent again first.

------------------------------------------------------------
 6. AFTER-READING  (operator completed setup, then this was taken)
------------------------------------------------------------
The operator completed setup at the handset and re-enabled USB debugging.
Readings taken 15:41-15:49, Tier A only, A1/A2 through the SAME product
path as the BEFORE reading (AdbApplicationInventoryProvider), so the two
counts are like-for-like. Enumeration completeness = COMPLETE.

  A1 total packages     334  ->  333   lower, fall non-zero     MET
  A2 third-party          5  ->    4   lower                    MET
  A3 CYVRA packages       1  ->    0   exactly 0                MET
  A4 CYVRA owner refs     3  ->    0   no reference             MET
  A5 bootreason   reboot,userrequested
                   -> reboot,factory_reset   contains factory_reset  MET

Boot instant pinned independently: host clock 15:49:29, uptime 2577.8 s
=> boot at 2026-10-14 15:06:32. The trigger was issued at approx 15:05, so
the device rebooted about 92 seconds after the command went out.

WHY THE BOOT INSTANT WAS MEASURED. `reboot,factory_reset` names the KIND of
boot, not who asked for it. By itself it would not attribute this reset to
our trigger - the operator had just been at the phone. Attribution rests on
the conjunction: the device left ADB the instant the trigger was issued, it
came back booted at 15:06:32, it had the agent as device owner with 3 owner
references before, and it has 0 CYVRA packages with 0 owner references now.
Any one of those alone would be weak. Together they are the measurement.

A1 AND A2 ARE NOT USER-DATA EVIDENCE, BY CONTRACT. The fall of exactly 1 in
A1 is the agent itself being removed - a package count, not a data removal.
Section 5 of the contract already says A1/A2 carry no weight alone and that
the conjunction with A5 is what carries the weight. Read that way here too.

------------------------------------------------------------
 7. VERDICT
------------------------------------------------------------
  PURGE_ATTESTED_MACHINE

Graded against section 4 of the ratified contract: every Tier A criterion
met, Tier B not taken. Not PURGE_ATTESTED_HYBRID, because Tier B was not
attested, and not upgraded to it by inference.

The section 4.1 trigger outcome, which read INDETERMINATE at the time, is
now settled by exactly the mechanism the contract names: the post-reboot
reading. The wipe did fire.

STILL NOT ESTABLISHED BY THIS RUN:
  - the full SANITIZE_EXECUTE protocol chain. It was not exercised: that
    path is gated on the entitlement flag, which is still off. What ran was
    the destructive provider execute() itself calls. The flag remains with
    the Chief Engineer.
  - Tier B (contacts, media, accounts). Not taken, by design. The operator
    was at the handset and can attest them directly, which would move the
    verdict to PURGE_ATTESTED_HYBRID; absent that attestation it stays at
    MACHINE.
  - agent-less purging. Nothing here is evidence for it. Section 5 of the
    contract forbids citing any of this to that route.
  - any device other than this one bench handset.
